WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.677
Two payment threads update one merchant balance, so each must take a lock first.

2
00:00:07.867 --> 00:00:11.397
The lock is one number in memory, and 0 means free.

3
00:00:11.397 --> 00:00:15.412
T1 takes it with a compare and swap: if it's 0, make it 1.

4
00:00:15.412 --> 00:00:18.873
One instruction, and the kernel never hears of it.

5
00:00:19.033 --> 00:00:21.830
T2's swap expects 0, finds 1, and fails.

6
00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:26.513
Rather than spin, it should sleep, and only the kernel can do that.

7
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:31.268
T2 sets the word to 2, taken with a waiter, and calls futex wait.

8
00:00:31.268 --> 00:00:35.204
The kernel checks it still says 2, and puts T2 to sleep.

9
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.383
T1 unlocks by swapping in 0.

10
00:00:37.383 --> 00:00:41.917
The old value was 2, so it makes a futex call to wake a sleeper.

11
00:00:41.917 --> 00:00:44.892
The kernel wakes T2, which takes the lock.

12
00:00:45.033 --> 00:00:48.555
With no waiters, the kernel never joins in.

13
00:00:48.555 --> 00:00:49.784
That's a futex.

14
00:00:49.784 --> 00:00:57.811
In the lab, a million uncontended locks made no futex calls, and four threads fighting made 2,749.

15
00:00:57.967 --> 00:00:59.000
Now two locks.

16
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.902
A refund moves 120 from the merchant to a customer, and a payment moves 80 back.

17
00:01:04.902 --> 00:01:06.968
Each locks its source first.

18
00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:09.044
The refund locks the merchant.

19
00:01:09.044 --> 00:01:12.236
At the same moment, the payment locks the customer.

20
00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:16.667
Now the refund wants the customer, so it sleeps on that lock's futex.

21
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:19.551
The payment wants the merchant, and sleeps too.

22
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.520
Each holds what the other needs: a deadlock.

23
00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:28.161
The kernel just sees two sleeping threads using no CPU, and nothing will ever wake them.

24
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:33.768
The fix: always take the smaller account id first, whichever way the money goes.

25
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.089
Now both start with ACC-1001.

26
00:01:36.089 --> 00:01:37.458
The refund gets it.

27
00:01:37.458 --> 00:01:40.484
The payment waits for it, holding nothing.

28
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:46.163
The refund takes the customer lock too, moves the money, and lets both go.

29
00:01:46.163 --> 00:01:50.173
Then the payment runs, and the merchant ends at 9,960.

30
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.708
A free lock costs one instruction, and only waiting reaches the kernel.

31
00:01:54.708 --> 00:01:58.868
Take locks in one fixed order, and the waiting can never loop back.
